# =============================================================================
# TechieBlog — Caddy site snippet
# =============================================================================
# Installed by .github/workflows/deploy.yml at /srv/caddy/sites/techieblog.caddy
# (= /etc/caddy/sites inside the `caddy` container, pulled in by the main
# Caddyfile's `import sites/*.caddy`).
#
# CREATE-ONLY. The pipeline installs this file only when it does NOT already
# exist on the server, and never reloads Caddy when it was skipped. A file that
# is already there — whether written by hand or by an earlier run — is
# AUTHORITATIVE. To change routing, edit /srv/caddy/sites/techieblog.caddy on
# the server directly and reload:
#
#     docker exec caddy caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#
# TLS is automatic: Caddy provisions and renews the Let's Encrypt certificate
# for both names below once the DNS A records resolve to this VPS. That is why
# the A records must exist BEFORE the first deploy — see deploy/SERVER-SETUP.md.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BLAZOR SERVER / WEBSOCKETS — Caddy's defaults are sufficient. No extra
# directives are needed, and none are added here.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TechieBlog is Blazor SERVER: after the first render every interaction travels
# over a long-lived SignalR WebSocket at /_blazor. A proxy that mishandles the
# upgrade produces the classic symptom — the page renders once, then every click
# does nothing and "Attempting to reconnect to the server" appears.
#
# Caddy v2's `reverse_proxy` handles this correctly out of the box:
#   * it forwards the Connection/Upgrade handshake and then streams the
#     connection bidirectionally, with no per-connection read/write timeout;
#   * it sets X-Forwarded-For / -Proto / -Host automatically (the app trusts
#     them via ForwardedHeaders__KnownNetworks__0 in the compose file);
#   * there is no buffering of the upgraded stream.
# Nothing equivalent to nginx's `proxy_http_version 1.1` +
# `proxy_set_header Upgrade/Connection` block is required.
#
# The ONE thing that can break the circuit here is response compression, so the
# `encode` directive below explicitly excludes /_blazor* — that keeps the
# long-polling fallback unbuffered on networks where WebSockets are blocked,
# while still compressing ordinary HTML/CSS/JS.
# =============================================================================

# www -> apex redirect (DOMAIN is an APEX domain).
# Permanent (308) and issued before any proxying, so www never reaches the app
# and only one canonical origin is ever indexed. This must match
# SiteSettings:BaseUrl in the compose file: https://techierathore.com
www.techierathore.com {
	redir https://techierathore.com{uri} permanent
}

techierathore.com {
	# Request matcher: everything EXCEPT the Blazor circuit endpoint.
	# (`encode`'s own `match` block is a RESPONSE matcher — status/header only —
	# so excluding a path has to be done with a named request matcher like this.)
	@notblazor not path /_blazor*
	encode @notblazor zstd gzip

	reverse_proxy techieblog:8080
}
